Health: SDD

Purpose:
  Defines health check model beyond simple 200 OK. Three levels
  of health determine whether a service can receive traffic.

HealthLevels:
  Liveness:
    Definition: Service is running
    Check: Process alive, basic functionality
    Failure: Restart service
    Example: HTTP /health/live → 200

  Readiness:
    Definition: Service can accept traffic
    Check: Dependencies available, cache warm, queue connected
    Failure: Remove from load balancer
    Example: HTTP /health/ready → 200 or 503

  DependencyHealth:
    Definition: Downstream dependencies are healthy
    Check: Database reachable, cache connected, queue available
    Failure: Mark service as degraded
    Example: PostgreSQL ping → OK, Redis ping → OK

HealthCheck:
  Required:
    - Liveness: Always
    - Readiness: Always for production
    - Dependency: For services with external dependencies

  Response:
    Healthy:
      status: healthy
      checks: { liveness: OK, readiness: OK, dependencies: OK }
    Degraded:
      status: degraded
      checks: { liveness: OK, readiness: OK, dependencies: DEGRADED }
    Unhealthy:
      status: unhealthy
      checks: { liveness: FAIL, readiness: FAIL, dependencies: FAIL }

CrossLayer:
  Architecture: Service dependency mapping
  Security: Health endpoints MUST be protected
  Operations: Health check routing and monitoring

